12:22:36 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Great question! There are two separate phenomena at play here, and the second one hinges on a common misconception about cabin pressure. ### Why it’s so dry The air at 35,000 feet is extremely cold (around -60°F / -50°C). Cold air can hold very little moisture—absolute humidity is near zero. When the plane draws in this outside air (through the engines’ bleed air system), the air is compressed and warmed to a comfortable temperature, but no moisture is added. This causes the **relative humidity** to plummet, often to as low as 5–15%—drier than most deserts. The recirculated cabin air is filtered but not re-humidified. So you’re effectively sitting in a very dry stream of air for hours, which dries out your skin, eyes, and respiratory tract. ### Why your ears pop (even if the cabin pressure “stays the same”) Here’s the key: **cabin pressure does *not* stay at sea-level pressure.** While the structure keeps the pressure from dropping to the outside level (which would be fatal), it’s only pressurized to an equivalent altitude of about 6,000–8,000 feet above sea level—similar to being on a mountain. As the plane climbs, the cabin pressure gradually decreases from the ground-level pressure you boarded at to that target “cabin altitude.” This reduction in air pressure creates a pressure difference across your eardrum: the higher pressure trapped in your middle ear pushes outward, causing the “pop” as it equalizes through the Eustachian tube. Once at cruise, the cabin pressure stabilizes at that lower value, so the sensation stops—until you descend and pressure rises again, making your ears pop in reverse. So the “pop” doesn’t mean the cabin pressure is fluctuating the whole flight; it happens because the cabin pressure *changed* from what you started with, and is held significantly lower than ground level.
