12:25:49 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a great clarification, and it gets to the heart of where the misunderstanding lies: **what exactly the pilot is “dialing in” before takeoff, and what the system does with that setting.** Your buddy the pilot is telling you the truth from their operational perspective, but it’s being interpreted differently by a non-pilot. Let me explain what’s really happening. ### The “one setting” the pilot dials in In most modern jets, before takeoff, the crew does set a target value on the pressurization control panel. This is usually the **landing field elevation** (the altitude of the destination airport) or the expected cruise altitude, depending on the system. They set it once, and for the rest of the flight, the pressurization is fully automatic—the pilots don’t touch it again unless there’s an abnormality. So your friend says, “I dial in one setting and it holds there the whole flight.” He means: **the target setting remains unchanged** for the whole flight, and the system automatically manages everything from that input. He is not saying the cabin pressure itself remains at that single value the entire time. ### What the automatic system does with that setting The pressurization controller uses that input (plus the current aircraft altitude from the flight management computer) to compute and execute a **cabin altitude schedule**. The schedule is a smooth curve: - **Takeoff:** The cabin starts at ground elevation pressure (or slightly pressurized to avoid a bump). - **Climb:** The controller gradually lets the cabin pressure decrease at a controlled rate (usually 300–500 feet per minute cabin altitude change) to a maximum preset cabin altitude (e.g., 8,000 ft) at the scheduled cruise altitude. - **Cruise:** Once at cruise, the controller actively holds the cabin altitude *constant* at that maximum value, using the outflow valve to compensate for minor fluctuations. - **Descent:** When the aircraft starts descending, the controller gradually increases cabin pressure again, aiming to reach the landing field elevation (the setting dialed in) just before landing. It does this smoothly so your ears aren’t hammered. So the pilot literally does set one number (landing field elevation), and the automatic system handles all the pressure changes—climb, cruise, descent—based on that. From the pilot’s perspective, they don’t manually change anything in flight, so they say “it just holds.” But what holds? The *target schedule holds*, not the cabin pressure itself. The cabin pressure is moving up and down, just automatically. ### Why the pilot’s statement feels contradictory It’s like setting a thermostat to a single temperature for the day: you dial in 72°F and leave it. The system heats, cools, and holds steady — but the temperature inside the house isn’t *always exactly 72°F from the moment you leave*; it might drop if it’s cold outside and then the furnace brings it back up. You “set it once” and it “holds” in the sense that the setpoint doesn’t change, but the actual temperature does vary as the system works to maintain it. In the airplane, the cabin pressure “varies” on a pre-planned curve, not randomly, but it is definitely changing during climb and descent. ### Reconciling your experience with your friend’s word Your ears prove it: they pop only during climb and descent. That means the cabin pressure is changing. Your friend says he dials in a setting and leaves it. Both are true: - The setting (landing elevation) is left unchanged. - The cabin pressure changes automatically to follow the schedule. - The pilot never needs to adjust anything once it’s set, so they think of it as “holding the whole flight.” It’s a classic case of a professional using shorthand that makes intuitive sense to them (“I set it and forget it”) but misleading to someone not familiar with the automatic schedule. So your friend isn’t wrong, and I’m not wrong—we’re just describing the same system from different angles. The cabin absolutely does move pressure-wise during climb and descent, but the pilot doesn’t have to manually command those changes because the controller does it all from that one initial setting.
