de. That desired pressure (or cabin altitude) is what changes during climb and descent. ### The “pressurized” label is still correct A plane cabin is pressurized because its internal pressure is actively kept **higher** than the outside air pressure at altitude. But “pressurized” does not mean “locked at a single value.” It means “maintained above ambient by an active system.” The system can vary that setpoint just like your home thermostat can change the temperature setpoint from 68°F at night to 72°F during the day—it’s still a climate-controlled house, just with a different target. ### This perfectly explains your friend and your ears Your pilot friend “sets one number” (the landing field elevation) and the automatic system uses it to compute a schedule: it gradually lowers cabin pressure from the departure field up to the cruise cabin altitude, then later raises it again to the landing field. The system is actively pumping in air and modulating outflow valves the entire time. From the pilot’s seat, it’s “set and forget” because the computer does the rest. But the pressure inside is absolutely changing—just like a dimmer switch smoothly changes light level even though you only set a single dial once. Your ears pop because that internal pressure is moving. It’s not a sealed soda can; it’s a smart, leaky balloon with a pump. And once at cruise, it holds that new pressure steady—no more pops. So your physical sensations and your buddy’s operational truth are both right, once you update the mental image from “sealed can” to “controlled leak.”
