g. The lack of sensation is proof of good engineering, not absence of air movement. This should finally reconcile their "soda can" intuition with the reality: a soda can is sealed, so no sound or drafts; a plane cabin is actively ventilated but the design makes it feel sealed because it's so well hidden. I'll structure the answer: acknowledge the intuition, then explain the hidden, silent design, compare the expected vs. actual experience, and tie it back to ear pops as the only perceptible clue.
