12:31:21 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a really good article to bring up—and it actually cuts to the heart of what modern aircraft are trying to achieve. But there’s an important nuance in what “basically sea-level pressure” means, and it doesn’t mean the cabin pressure never changes from gate to gate. Let me clarify. ### The 787’s cabin altitude is lower, not “sea level constant” The Boeing 787 does *not* maintain the cabin at actual sea-level pressure (0 feet cabin altitude) throughout the flight. What articles are describing is that its cabin altitude is typically around **6,000 feet** at cruise, compared to the 8,000 feet common in older aluminum aircraft. Why the confusion? Because 6,000 feet is *closer* to sea level than 8,000 feet—so journalists and marketing materials often shorthand this as “near sea-level pressure.” But 6,000 feet is still well above sea level. At 6,000 feet, the air pressure is about 11.8 psi, vs. 14.7 psi at sea level. That’s a very noticeable difference—your ears would still pop if you drove from the beach up to a 6,000-foot mountain town. So the 787 is more comfortable, but it does not eliminate the pressure change; it just reduces its magnitude. ### Even if it were sea-level pressure, it wouldn’t be constant airport to airport Let’s play out the ideal scenario you’re imagining: Suppose an aircraft could structurally maintain an internal pressure of exactly 14.7 psi (sea level) from gate to gate, regardless of outside altitude. Even then, the pressure wouldn’t be “constant” in the way you experience it if you’re flying between airports at different elevations. - If you departed from a sea-level airport, the cabin starts at 14.7 psi. - At cruise, it holds 14.7 psi (sea level) instead of a lower pressure. Excellent. - But if your destination is Denver (5,280 feet, about 12.2 psi), the cabin must eventually *reduce pressure* during descent to match the outside pressure at the gate, or else you’d feel a sudden pressure increase when the door opens. So the cabin pressure would still have to change during descent—just from sea level down to Denver’s pressure. Your ears would pop. - The reverse is true flying Denver → sea level: the cabin would start at Denver’s lower pressure, and during climb it would have to *increase* to sea level pressure. Ears pop again. So a fully rigid “sea level only” setting would still require pressure adjustments to match the destination, meaning the pressure is not frozen from start to finish. ### The real goal: smaller, smoother changes The “goal” in aircraft design isn’t to have a single unchanging pressure. It’s to: 1. Keep the cabin altitude as low as structurally practical, minimizing the initial drop from your departure elevation. 2. Make any pressure changes extremely slow and gentle, so your ears barely notice. 3. End the flight at the landing airport’s current pressure. The 787’s 6,000-foot cabin altitude is a step in that direction. Combined with a very smooth controller, it’s common for passengers to not notice any ear pops at all on a 787, or only a very subtle one. But that’s because the *magnitude* of the change is smaller and the rate is perfectly controlled, not because the pressure is a flat line from takeoff to touchdown. ### Does this support “pressure staying the same is the goal”? Only in the sense that remaining *close* to your starting pressure during cruise is a goal for comfort. But a flat, static pressure that never changes is neither possible (due to structural limits and destination elevation differences) nor desirable (you’d need to match the landing airport anyway). The 787’s design proves that the industry is trying to make the pressure drop *smaller*—which tells you there is indeed a drop to minimize. If they could hold a perfect sea-level pressure constantly, they would market it as “zero cabin altitude” and it would be a massive breakthrough. That hasn’t happened yet, because the weight and structural cost would be enormous. ### Back to your own ears
